(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Besides age over 65, which finding belongs to the criteria for diagnosing idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis without biopsy?","choices":["Elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme","Bilateral hilar adenopathy","Restrictive physiology on testing","Obstructive physiology on testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The four criteria are idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles on examination, restrictive physiology on pulmonary function testing, radiographic evidence of progressive fibrosis over several years, and diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 44, 45"},{"q":"Which finding in sarcoidosis is an indication for oral corticosteroids?","choices":["Hilar adenopathy alone","Hypercalcemia","Erythema nodosum","Elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Indications for oral corticosteroids in sarcoidosis include disabling constitutional symptoms, hypercalcemia, iritis, uveitis, arthritis, central nervous system or cardiac involvement, granulomatous hepatitis, cutaneous lesions other than erythema nodosum and progressive pulmonary lesions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 47, 48"},{"q":"Which drug is both an immunosuppressant used in sarcoidosis and a listed cause of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Infliximab","Amiodarone","Methotrexate","Azathioprine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is one of the immunosuppressants most commonly used when sarcoidosis is corticosteroid-intolerant or refractory, and it treats many autoimmune diseases such as lupus and rheumatoid arthritis. It is also implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease in the drug list and the treatment-related box. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 18, 46, 72"},{"q":"Which measure belongs to the supportive care of an adult with coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":["Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines","Lavage to remove the coal dust","Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Higher hemoglobin targets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care for coal workers' pneumoconiosis includes oxygen, pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, because no effective curative treatment is available. Lung transplant may be indicated in severe cases. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 56, 74"},{"q":"In which patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome does early prone positioning decrease mortality?","choices":["Mild cases","Cases with cardiomegaly","Patients in recovery","Moderate to severe cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early prone positioning decreases mortality in moderate to severe acute respiratory distress syndrome, that is, a ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to inspired oxygen under 200 mm Hg. It is added to low tidal volume ventilation and treatment of the precipitant. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Which imaging finding is characteristic of silicosis?","choices":["Pleural plaques at the bases","Patchy pleural-based honeycombing","Normal heart with air bronchograms","Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis imaging shows nodular opacities throughout, silicotic nodules of 1 to 10 mm forming nodular pulmonary fibrosis, and eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"Over what period does pulmonary function generally recover after acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["1 to 2 months","6 to 12 months","18 to 24 months","36 to 48 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary function improves over 6 to 12 months, generally recovering within about a year, though functional deficits often remain. Survivors need monitoring for residual diffusion deficits and treatment of post-intensive care muscle wasting, weakness and fatigue. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 74"},{"q":"Which sarcoidosis finding is the stated exception among the cutaneous indications for oral corticosteroids?","choices":["Parotid gland enlargement","Erythema nodosum","Lupus pernio","Lesions on the back and arms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous lesions other than erythema nodosum are an indication for oral corticosteroids, so erythema nodosum, the itchy and painful rash on the lower legs and ankles, is the exception. Lupus pernio and other skin lesions still count toward treatment. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 47"},{"q":"Which intervention offers no clinical benefit when used routinely in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Early prone positioning","Pulmonary artery catheter","Conservative fluid protocol","Low tidal volume ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Routine pulmonary artery catheters offer no clinical benefit in acute respiratory distress syndrome and are avoided. Cardiac function is assessed instead with echocardiography to exclude hydrostatic cardiogenic pulmonary edema. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Which description fits pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":["Permanent scar that stiffens the lung","Reversible swelling of the airways","Overinflated, overly compliant lung","Malignant growth in the alveoli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis is a response to an inflammatory source that lays down permanent scar tissue, making the lung tissue stiff and impairing gas exchange. The scarring damages the alveoli, most often permanently, and the stiff, restrictive lung causes shortness of breath. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 4, 5, 6"},{"q":"Which imaging study is preferred for asbestosis?","choices":["Plain chest radiograph","Echocardiography","Pleural fluid cytology","Computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography is the preferred imaging for asbestosis. It can show pleural plaques, alveolitis and fibrosis, with honeycombing in advanced disease, while the plain chest radiograph is often normal, especially early in the disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 58, 59, 73"},{"q":"In interstitial lung disease, what does a positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) test suggest?","choices":["Collagen vascular disease","Active sarcoidosis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Goodpasture syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, formerly Wegener's, a granulomatous vasculitis that is one of the interstitial lung diseases with a granulomatous response. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 25, 44"},{"q":"What size range do silicotic nodules span on imaging?","choices":["Under 1 mm","1 to 10 mm","2 to 5 mm","Over 10 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis shows nodular opacities throughout the lungs, silicotic nodules of 1 to 10 mm, a nodular pulmonary fibrosis, together with eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 60, 63"},{"q":"What is mesothelioma?","choices":["A cancer of the pleural lining","A benign pleural plaque","A scar of the terminal bronchioles","An infection of the pleural space"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mesothelioma is primarily a pleural cancer, a cancer of the pleural lining, and is nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, whose fibers can penetrate further into the lung. Patients frequently develop pleural effusions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 58, 59"},{"q":"What share of patients with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and familial lung fibrosis have a history of smoking?","choices":["20%","40 to 80%","67 to 75%","90%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Between 67 and 75% of patients with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and familial lung fibrosis have a history of smoking. Smoking is the cause in the majority of cases of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia, and cessation is critical to prolong life. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 35, 37, 44, 46, 48"},{"q":"Which test confirms the diagnosis of sarcoidosis?","choices":["Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme","Chest radiograph staging","Serum calcium level","Transbronchial lung or node biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transbronchial biopsy of the lung or fine-needle node biopsy confirms sarcoidosis by showing noncaseating granulomas. Biopsy is essential whenever clinical and radiographic findings suggest the possibility of an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 47"},{"q":"After microscopic lung injury, what leads to pulmonary fibrosis rather than lung homeostasis?","choices":["The type of stimulus","A single large injury","Aberrant wound healing","Intact wound healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exogenous and endogenous stimuli cause microscopic lung injury, separated spatially and temporally. When wound healing is intact the lung returns to homeostasis; when it is aberrant, with genetic predisposition and autoimmune conditions acting on it, the result is pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does cigarette smoking change the risks of asbestos exposure?","choices":["It lowers the risk of mesothelioma","It leaves the radiograph unchanged","It speeds clearance of the fibers","It sharply raises lung cancer risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In asbestos exposure, cigarette smoking increases the prevalence of radiographic pleural and parenchymal changes and markedly increases the incidence of lung carcinoma; the increased risk of lung cancer and mesothelioma applies especially to smokers. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 55, 56"},{"q":"Which lung response do the connective tissue diseases such as lupus and rheumatoid arthritis produce?","choices":["Inflammatory and fibrotic","Noncaseating granulomatous","Malignant transformation","Opportunistic infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Connective tissue diseases (think autoimmune) such as systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis and Sjögren's syndrome produce an inflammatory and fibrotic lung response, the same group of response as asbestos, drugs and radiation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 17, 18, 20"},{"q":"A ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen of 80 mm Hg places acute respiratory distress syndrome in which severity class?","choices":["Outside the criterion","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen under 100 mm Hg is severe acute respiratory distress syndrome; 100 to 200 is moderate and 200 to 300 mild. Severe, refractory cases are where extracorporeal membrane oxygenation is reserved as salvage. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"When may the physical examination be normal in interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Early in its course","With advanced fibrosis","Once dyspnea occurs at rest","With right heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Early in interstitial lung disease the examination may be normal. As the disease advances, small lung volumes, bibasilar dry rales or inspiratory crackles and distal clubbing appear, and right heart failure can develop with advanced disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 24"},{"q":"Which reticular pattern on chest radiograph is an early finding in interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Coarse, cystic spaces","Reticulonodular overlap","Fine, ground-glass","Medium, honeycombing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reticular, curvilinear opacities are graded fine, medium and coarse: the fine, ground-glass pattern is an early finding, medium honeycombing is a late finding, and coarse opacities are cystic spaces. Chest films also show low lung volumes with patchy opacities. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which group makes up the most common interstitial lung diseases of known etiology?","choices":["Idiopathic interstitial pneumonias","Infections of the lung","Occupational or environmental exposures","Connective tissue diseases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common interstitial lung diseases of known etiology come from occupational or environmental exposures: inorganic dusts, organic dusts, and fumes or gases. When a known cause is suspected, the exposure history at work and in the environment is where it is found. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 13"},{"q":"Over what time course do most interstitial lung diseases present?","choices":["In discrete episodes","Months to years","Days to weeks","Weeks to months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most interstitial lung diseases present chronically, over months to years; idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis, pneumoconiosis and connective tissue diseases present this way. Acute, subacute and episodic presentations are the less usual patterns. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In which setting should idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia be suspected?","choices":["Serum antibodies to an inhaled agent","Bilateral hilar adenopathy alone","Unexplained interstitial lung disease","Known heavy asbestos exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia should be suspected in a patient with unexplained interstitial lung disease; it is the most common diagnosis among patients with interstitial lung disease, and when no associated cause is evident the pattern is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 35, 45, 55, 69"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph finding points to sarcoidosis or silicosis among the interstitial lung diseases?","choices":["Hilar and mediastinal adenopathy","Pleural plaques at the bases","Normal heart, air bronchograms","Pleural-based honeycombing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On chest radiography, hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy points to sarcoidosis or silicosis. Sarcoidosis shows symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal adenopathy, and silicosis can calcify the periphery of the hilar nodes in an eggshell pattern. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 34, 44, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"Which test helps exclude cardiogenic pulmonary edema when acute respiratory distress syndrome is suspected?","choices":["Pulmonary artery catheter","Bronchoalveolar lavage","Pulmonary function testing","Echocardiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic assessment evaluates cardiac function to exclude hydrostatic cardiogenic pulmonary edema, the main differential. Supporting this, the heart size is normal in acute respiratory distress syndrome, with minimal or absent pleural effusions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 62, 73, 74"},{"q":"Which medication is used for sarcoidosis that is intolerant of or refractory to corticosteroids?","choices":["Azathioprine","Amiodarone","Nitrofurantoin","Bronchodilators"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunosuppressive medications, most commonly methotrexate, azathioprine or infliximab, are used in patients with sarcoidosis who are intolerant of corticosteroids or who have corticosteroid-refractory disease. About 90% of cases otherwise respond to corticosteroids. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 46, 71, 72"},{"q":"Which finding gives a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?","choices":["Little change on acute imaging","Serum antibodies to the agent","White cell count with left shift","Restrictive pulmonary function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies to the agent in serum give a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Supporting findings include an elevated white blood cell count with a left shift, hypoxia and restrictive pulmonary function, and lung biopsy may be indicated in subacute or chronic disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 57, 69"},{"q":"What do macrophages, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils accumulating in the alveolar walls first produce?","choices":["Alveolitis","Collagen deposition","Honeycombing","Granulomas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the pathophysiology of fibrosis, macrophages, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils accumulate in the alveolar walls and alveolitis occurs. Alveolitis then leads to fibroblast production, and the collagen those fibroblasts produce causes the fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 20, 26"}]};
